From 6375b18becafb192be749248579174c05e16078b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexis Williams <alexis@typedr.at>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:36:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] Give WebAssembly time zones, a memory monitor, content
 types and a user

Where "Add support for WebAssembly" gives a platform service no backend,
or one that falls short, GLib now answers on WebAssembly as it does
elsewhere.

Time zones come from Intl. WebAssembly skipped every TZif path, so only
fixed offsets and POSIX rule strings worked, IANA names such as
"America/Recife" always failed, and the default zone was hardcoded to
"UTC0". A page has no zoneinfo files, and shipping tzdata would
duplicate what the JavaScript engine already has: Intl knows every IANA
zone. The TZif data for an identifier is now synthesized from Intl and
handed to GLib's usual TZif parser, and the default zone is the one Intl
resolves for the page or process. TZ is still honoured first. An
identifier Intl doesn't know fails as a missing file does. Intl accepts
names in any case; such a name is known by Intl's spelling, so
"europe/berlin" is the zone "Europe/Berlin". Aliases keep the name they
were asked for, since Intl maps them to other zones' names.

Transitions are listed from 1800 to 2100: offsets are sampled weekly and
each change is narrowed down to the second, so changes less than a week
apart that cancel out are missed. After 2100 a POSIX TZ footer repeats
the 2090s' pattern when it is a fixed offset or a yearly
month-week-weekday rule, and the zone keeps its last offset otherwise. A
period counts as daylight saving time when its offset is above the
lowest offset in both the year before and the year after it began.
Abbreviations are Intl's short English names where alphabetic ("PST"),
else numeric ("+0100") or "UTC". Synthesizing a zone takes tens of
milliseconds, so the data is cached per identifier for good.

g_memory_monitor_dup_default() returned NULL, and passed it to
g_object_ref(): the D-Bus, portal, PSI and poll monitors aren't
registered on WebAssembly. A monitor that never warns, since nothing
reports memory pressure there, is now registered as the fallback.

g_content_type_is_a() only compared types for equality. Every type but
an inode is now also an application/octet-stream, as with the
shared-mime-info backend.

Emscripten's getpwnam_r(), getpwuid_r() and getpwuid() are stubs that
report ENOENT, so the first g_get_user_name(), g_get_real_name() or
g_get_home_dir() without HOME warned that the user id was unknown, a
fatal warning in tests. The lookup is skipped, and the fallbacks its
failure led to anyway are used: "somebody" and "Unknown", and no home
directory from the user database.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Wasm-specific]
